--[[
        NOISE 90 -- every "-90" as a Noise. One script, all of them: pick one with _G.Type

        A TV shows up in the room you are in. Something sets its tape off (each one its own way:
        you stare at it, you walk up to it, you look away, you open a drawer, you put a disc in);
        the tape plays, tinted in its colour, with its face in the middle of the picture, and
        Noise comes out of the screen in its colours. Then each one does its own thing.

            _G.Type = "B-90"     -- which one (the list below)
            _G.Setup = "Auto"    -- where its TV is (the list below)
            _G.Disc = "Look"     -- B-90 only: "Look" or "DontLook" (with the disc player, the disc you put in decides)
            _G.RandomPos = true  -- the TV anywhere in the room, on a battery (optional)
            loadstring(game:HttpGet("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bonellocristian78-lab/Gradish-Mode/main/Noise90"))()

        Run it again and the one before stays: they go on together (getgenv().__Noise90.stop() ends them all). The kits
        and the files are read once; the next ones take them as they are, so a new one does not stop the game.

        THE TYPES (faces, colours and ways from the wikis: Interminable Rooms fanmade, Interminable Rooms, INTERMINABLE FEVER)
            B-90           Noise with B-90 in it. Stare at the TV and it comes out and walks your steps; it can be
                           crucified (B-90 is torn out of it) and the TV can be broken with the hammer. Its disc comes
                           in two: "Look" (stare at the tape) and "Don't Look" (the tape plays: do not look at it)
            C-90           The contrary one: its tape only plays while you look away from it; out, it walks only
                           while you stand still, and stops as soon as you move
            Ransom//90     Red, Ransom in the middle of its tape. It sleeps in the TV: every drawer you open, it
                           climbs out and comes for you; it hits you for 90 and walks backwards into its TV
            A-90           The wiki's green-yellow face. 10 rebounds: the room goes dark; each time its face on your
                           screen (freeze, or 90), then out of the TV across the room and back faster than you can
                           follow, every locker it passes thrown about: if you are in one, it has you
            X-90           The wiki's cyan face: 40 rebounds, four at a time, 50 times faster; lockers and tables
            G-90           Two dark blue eyes and a smile. Out of a paper (it checks the tables), or out of a
                           computer (it checks the tables, fakes it is gone for 5 seconds, then checks the lockers)
            Pix-90         The wiki's three pixel frames. It creeps closer in pixel jumps; its face pops up: move
                           while it is there and its mouth opens wider; the third time it jumps at you (90)
            Trollface-90   Its face on your screen: stop moving, or the Rage Guy jumps all over it, 90, and you do
                           not heal any more. Pass and it turns red, then white, and goes with A-90's sound
            XTrollface-90  The opposite: inverted, bloodshot, a giggle first, it flashes yellow: if you do NOT move,
                           it hits you for 90 to 100
            S-90           INTERMINABLE FEVER's: the room goes dark, its TV glows one colour; its face comes up with
                           its buttons (redn, geen, bule) while three of it circle you: click its colour in time.
                           Wrong: "LEARN THE COLORS OF THE RAINBOW. FOOL." and you are kicked
            XS-90          Inverted and angry, red pupils: six colours (yell, magnet, cyn too), much less time;
                           or run to the nearest hiding place
            X-60           Its own tape: its six yellow faces, two orbiting the middle one. Then it goes off the way
                           you came and rushes back, faster and faster, loud static, quiet screams; it stays 9
                           seconds in your room, slowly going down. Hide
            A-404          Interminable Rooms' glitched one: a black hollow circle for a head on its static. Docile
                           (blue), it stands between you and the way on, in your way: "This is a glitched server,
                           please join a new one." and it goes in 20 to 40 seconds. Try to get past it and it is
                           angered (red): its head and its face come apart on your screen and it kicks you
                           (ERROR CODE 404), hiding or not
        Every one is in its own colours, body and all (no more of the kit's blue but on B-90).
        Every one can be crucified: a crucifix in your inventory, and in your hand when it is about to get you (it catches
        you, its face on your screen, S-90's wrong colour, A-404's kick) it is chained, rewound and goes down into its
        circle, in its colour, instead. Only from B-90's Noise is B-90 torn out, into a second circle.

        THE SETUPS (Auto: the type's own, at random, "Cart" being any of the four carts; in the Archives every screen there is)
            Ceiling        above the door, hanging from the ceiling on its arm (the hammer works here)
            Table          on a table against the far wall: turn its antenna
            Cart           the Stairwell's TV cart with its disc player: find the disc, put it in;
                           once it is out, hold "Eject" and the disc pulls it back into its TV.
                           The discs come in their cases: front cover, back cover (the wiki's words and how to
                           survive), spine, the disc and its label. Taken, a disc is an item in your inventory (its
                           cover is its icon): hold it to put it in. Click or right-click it in your hand (or Inspect,
                           R, on the floor) to look at it close: turn it, zoom, open the case, flip it. One may be
                           scratched (the tape skips and what comes out is angrier). The player: its tray, its
                           display (NO DISC, READ, PLAY 00:07, PAUSE, EJECT), its Pause (F) while the tape plays
            AVCart         the disc player on a school AV cart: grey steel, three shelves, the TV strapped on top
            Console        the disc player in a 70s walnut TV console: open its doors first
            Luggage        the disc player on the hotel's brass luggage cart: the TV on a suitcase, the player on a
                           vanity case
            Pedestal       a pedestal by the far wall and the TV on the floor: pick it up, put it there, turn its
                           antenna. The pedestal may give way: the TV falls, cracks, and keeps going
            Teller         the Teller's screen
            Computer       a computer on a table (G-90's)
            Screens        every screen in the room plays the tape, and Noise comes out of them in pieces
                           (a leg from one, an arm from another) that come together
        The TV is on as it is. RandomPos: the TV (its table, its cart, its pedestal) anywhere in the room instead
        of by the far wall, on a battery: a 6 V battery lies in the room; take it and put it in the box on its side.
        With RandomPos, Auto never takes the ceiling or the room's own screens (B-90: one of the carts).
        THE NOISE JOURNAL: a book in your inventory (its icon, every run). Every disc you find is written in it and
        kept in the executor's folder (Noise/Journal.txt) from one run to the next: open it to read the page of each
        one (its picture, what it is, how to survive, when you found it); the ones you have not found are ???.

        Every setting, with its default. All optional, before the loadstring:

            _G.Noise90 = {
                Time = 0,               -- seconds after running before the TV appears
                TVScale = 0,            -- the TV's size (1 = the kit's; 0 = a little bigger or smaller each time)
                LookTime = 3.5,         -- seconds you must look at the TV
                LookAngle = 14,         -- degrees from the middle of your view
                LookDistance = 70,      -- studs
                NearDistance = 18,      -- studs: the types that start when you come close
                FollowDoors = true,     -- the TV moves to each new room until it starts
                SpeedOffset = -0.1,     -- its speed = yours + this
                StillSlowdown = 3.5,    -- this much slower while you stand still
                FarDistance = 70,       -- studs along your path: farther, it screams and rushes
                CloseDistance = 25,     -- it rushes until it is this close again
                Rush = 8,               -- rushing speed = yours + this
                ScreamCooldown = 6,     -- seconds between two screams
                KillDistance = 4.5,     -- studs
                EmergeSpeed = 1.4,      -- how fast it crawls out of the TV (its animation is 8.5 s)
                ChaseRooms = 3,         -- DOORS: it ends after this many new rooms
                ChaseTime = 90,         -- it ends after this many seconds anyway
                Rounds = 0,             -- rebounds, faces or colours (0 = the type's own)
                PedestalFall = 0.5,     -- the chance the pedestal gives way
                RandomPos = false,      -- the TV anywhere in the room, on a battery (also _G.RandomPos = true)
                Now = false,            -- it comes out at once: no waiting, no tape, no battery, no antenna, no
                                        -- pedestal; its discs written in the Journal as it comes (Noise90All's way)
                Kick = true,            -- S-90 kicks you out of the game (false: it kills you)
                DiscWear = true,        -- a disc may be scratched (it skips); false: always clean;
                                        -- or "scratched", "clean" for every disc
                Laugh = "",             -- a sound id for Trollface-90's laugh (A-90's sound, faster, without one)
                G90 = "",               -- "Paper" or "Computer" (empty: either)
                Hammer = true,          -- a hammer on the floor to break the TV (Ceiling)
                CrucifixGive = true,    -- a crucifix in your inventory (also _G.CrucifixGive = true / false)
                Volume = 1,
                Folder = "Noise",       -- where the kits, the tape and the faces are kept
                AssetsUrl = "...",      -- where they are downloaded from
            }

        The kits (the TV, Noise, the props of the Stairwell and the Archives), the tape and the faces
        are downloaded once into the executor's folder and loaded from there: nothing is uploaded,
        only you see and hear it. Without a kit, plain pieces stand in.
]]

